.TH DINGDONGDITCH 1 2026-11-01 "DingDongDitch 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dingdongditch \- find open ports on hosts you own, and work out what is behind them
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dingdongditch scan
.RI [ options ]
.I target
.RI [ target ...]
.br
.B dingdongditch analyze
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I report
.br
.B dingdongditch update\-db
.br
.B dingdongditch
.RB [ \-V | \-\-version ]
.RB [ \-\-help ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dingdongditch
knocks on the TCP ports of a host, asks each open port what it is, and reports
the service, product and version it found.
With
.B \-\-karen\-mode
it also reads web pages and TLS certificates, guesses the operating system,
and proves common misconfigurations, which it marks
.BR CONFIRMED .
.PP
It detects and never exploits.
Only scan systems you own or have written permission to test.
.PP
The risk model is not built yet, so the Risk column says
.I model not trained yet
unless a finding is
.BR CONFIRMED .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B scan
Scan one or more targets and print what was found.
.TP
.B analyze
Read an nmap XML report made with
.B nmap \-sV \-oX
and print it the same way.
Give
.B \-
as
.I report
to read from standard input.
.TP
.B update\-db
Download CVE data.
Not implemented yet: it prints a message and exits with status 1.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Scan options
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-engine " " \fIengine\fR
Which scanner to use:
.B own
(the built-in scanner, the default) or
.B nmap
(needs nmap installed).
.TP
.BR \-P ", " \-\-profile " " \fIprofile\fR
How many ports to scan:
.BR quick ,
.B default
or
.BR full .
See
.BR PORTS .
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-ports " " \fIports\fR
Scan exactly these ports, for example
.B 22,80,443
or
.B 8000\-9000
or both together.
This wins over
.B \-\-profile
and over the all-ports default of
.BR \-\-karen\-mode .
.TP
.B \-\-karen\-mode
Demand to speak to every port's manager.
See
.BR "KAREN MODE" .
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-timeout " " \fIseconds\fR
Built-in scanner only.
How long to wait for each port to accept a connection.
Default 1.0, minimum 0.05.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-concurrency " " \fIcount\fR
Built-in scanner only.
How many ports to try at once.
Default 500, minimum 1.
Lowered automatically if the system runs out of sockets.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-save " " \fIfile\fR
Also save the raw results to
.IR file :
JSON with the built-in scanner, nmap XML with the nmap engine.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print JSON instead of a table.
Only the JSON goes to standard output, so it can be piped.
Also accepted by
.BR analyze .
.SS General options
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Show help and exit.
Works after a command too.
.SH TARGETS
A target can be an IP address, a hostname or a subnet in CIDR form.
Separate several with spaces or commas.
Duplicates are removed.
A subnet can have at most 65,536 addresses (a /16), so a typo cannot start a
huge scan.
.SH PORTS
.TP
.B quick
107 hand-picked common and risky ports.
.TP
.B default
Ports 1\-1024 plus the quick list, 1,094 ports in all.
.TP
.B full
All 65,535 TCP ports.
.PP
With
.BR "\-\-engine nmap" ,
.B quick
and
.B default
are nmap's own top 100 and top 1000 ports instead.
.SH KAREN MODE
.B \-\-karen\-mode
scans all 65,535 ports unless
.B \-p
is given.
For each open port it also:
.IP \(bu 2
tries more probes on ports the first probe did not identify, over plain text
and TLS
.IP \(bu 2
fetches the home page of web ports for the title, telling headers and
application
.IP \(bu 2
reads TLS certificates and flags ones that are expired, not yet valid,
self-signed or weakly signed, and old TLS versions
.IP \(bu 2
guesses each host's operating system from clues in the banners, and shows
which port gave each clue
.IP \(bu 2
runs the checks in
.B CONFIRMED CHECKS
.PP
The operating system guess is only a guess.
Banners can be wrong or changed by the owner.
.PP
With
.BR "\-\-engine nmap" ,
this flag only scans all ports and uses every nmap version probe.
The extras above come from the built-in scanner.
.SH CONFIRMED CHECKS
Each check does the smallest exchange that proves the problem, then leaves.
Nothing is guessed, listed, read or changed.
.TP
.B ftp\-anonymous
Anyone can log in to the FTP server.
Logs in as the public
.B anonymous
user and quits straight away.
.TP
.B open\-shell
A port gives a command prompt with no login.
Recognised from the prompt it sends.
Nothing is ever typed into it.
.TP
.B redis\-no\-password
Redis answers without a password.
Uses the read-only
.B INFO server
reply that banner grabbing already asked for.
.TP
.B mongodb\-no\-auth
MongoDB answers without a login.
Asks for the list of databases, filtered to a made-up name so that no real
names come back.
.TP
.B telnet\-open
Telnet is running, which sends everything unencrypted.
Proved by its greeting, without logging in.
.SH OUTPUT
The table goes to standard output.
Warnings and progress go to standard error.
.PP
.B \-\-json
prints a list with one object per open port.
Its fields are
.BR host ,
.BR port ,
.BR protocol ,
.BR state ,
.BR name ,
.BR product ,
.BR version ,
.BR extrainfo ,
.BR cpes ,
.BR banner ,
.BR tls ,
.BR http_title ,
.BR http_headers ,
.BR tls_cert ,
.BR os_guess ,
.B os_evidence
and
.BR findings .
Each finding has
.BR check ,
.BR title ,
.BR why ,
.B fix
and
.BR status .
.SH MISSING PORTS
Going too fast can make open ports look closed, especially inside a virtual
machine or over a slow link.
If a scan finds fewer ports than expected, slow it down:
.RS
.PP
.B dingdongditch scan
.I target
.B \-c 100 \-t 3
.RE
.PP
Ports that time out get a second, slower try.
This is skipped when more than 5,000 ports time out, which is normal behind a
firewall, and the scan prints a warning.
.PP
Silent FTP and mail ports are given up to 12 seconds to greet, because some
servers delay their greeting.
.SH LIMITATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
TCP only, so UDP services such as SNMP are not seen.
.IP \(bu 2
It makes full TCP connections, which the target can log.
.IP \(bu 2
Services that wait for the client to speak in their own protocol, such as
PostgreSQL, DNS and SMB, are named from their port number with no version.
.IP \(bu 2
Versions come from what a service says about itself, and Linux distributions
often patch old versions without changing the number.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including a scan that found no open ports.
.TP
.B 1
The scan failed, a file was not found or is not an nmap report, or the command
is not implemented yet.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was wrong.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B dingdongditch scan 192.168.195.130
Scan the default ports of one host.
.TP
.B dingdongditch scan 192.168.195.130 \-p 8000\-9000
Scan a range of ports.
.TP
.B dingdongditch scan 192.168.195.0/24 \-P quick
Scan a whole subnet quickly.
.TP
.B dingdongditch scan 10.0.0.1,10.0.0.2
Scan two hosts.
.TP
.B dingdongditch scan 192.168.195.130 \-\-karen\-mode
Scan every port and run every safe check.
.TP
.B dingdongditch scan 192.168.195.130 \-\-json \-o scan.json
Print JSON and save it to a file.
.TP
.B dingdongditch scan 192.168.195.130 \-e nmap \-o scan.xml
Scan with nmap and keep its XML report.
.TP
.B dingdongditch analyze scan.xml
Read that report later.
.TP
.B nmap \-sV \-oX \- 192.168.195.130 | dingdongditch analyze \-
Read a report straight from nmap.
